  "opening": "In Chapter 2, we built reversible circuits that compute Boolean functions without losing information. In every step we have considered so far, each configuration has a single, definite next configuration. Not all transitions work this way: a step may allow several next configurations, or choose among them with probabilities.",
  "opening_continuation": "We first compare deterministic, nondeterministic, and probabilistic transitions. We then see why probabilities cannot describe every physical transition, leading to probability amplitudes and interference.",
  "intro_continuation": "In a quantum process, however, a single step on a bit initialized to \\(0\\) can produce \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) each, yet applying that exact same step a second time returns the bit to \\(0\\) with certainty. To understand how two such steps can undo each other's randomness, we begin by examining how single-step transitions are described and combined."
}
